# Front Door skins

## What's in
The four looks from `skins_library.zip` — Minimal Neutral, Bold Contrast, Warm Editorial, Soft Rounded — plus **Original** (the app's own look, untouched).

Each look gets its own accent per category, worked out from the category's name the same way the zip's `design_tokens.py` does it. So all 52 openapps.pro categories have all four looks. No colours were picked by hand.

## How a person uses it
Tap the round icon on their app. A row of five looks sits at the top of the sheet, each drawn in its own colours.

- Tap one → the app changes **straight away** as a preview → "Keep it" or "Not that".
- "Not that", closing the sheet, or typing something else → it goes back.
- They can also just say it: "make it dark", "more classic". The model picks the look and lists it back for approval.
- Colour, font and corner changes sit on top of whatever look is on. Asking for a colour while on Original adds a visible "switches to Minimal Neutral" line to the approval list first — never done silently.
- Every kept change is in the list with Undo.

## Where to change things (RULES block at the top of `front-door.html`)
- `LOOK_ON_LOAD` — what an app wears the moment it opens. `"original"` now.
- `LIBRARY` — each entry now takes `family`:
  - `"shared_card"` or `"todomvc"` → the app gets the full stylesheet (these are the two page layouts the zip measured).
  - `""` → the app gets colours and fonts only. Nothing is guessed at a page layout nobody has measured.

## Putting the skin into a real app (`frontdoor-skin-adapter.js`)
1. Add `<script src="frontdoor-skin-adapter.js"></script>` to the end of the app's page. Nothing else in the app changes.
2. Open the app through the Front Door and pick a look.
3. First time, nothing will happen and the browser console will say `ignored a skin from https://…`. Copy that address into `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` in the adapter's RULES block. Reload. Done.

The adapter only ever adds one `<style>` tag, one font link and `--fd-*` colour variables. Original takes them all off. Delete the script line and the app is exactly as it was.

## Tested (real Chromium, `test_skins.py`)
**86/86**, plus the earlier **54/54** still passing.
- All 172 skin files in the zip reproduced **byte for byte** by the page's own code.
- Every possible accent (all 360) × 4 looks = 1440 token sets identical to the Python.
- Preview, keep, not-that, undo, reload, close-mid-preview — desktop, mobile, mobile dark.
- Adapter: skin crosses to a page on another address, strips off on Original, refuses any other sender.

Broken on purpose four ways (wrong rounding, adapter open to anyone, "Not that" not restoring, the maths bug below) — the tests caught every one.

Bug found and fixed along the way: at one accent (hue 210, Soft Rounded) a button shade came out one step off the Python. Cause was how the remainder was taken. Now exact across the whole range.

## Not yet proven
- The adapter test used a plain test page, not one of your shelf apps. The first real proof is the first admitted app.
- Your new shelf apps (Gitea, Superset, etc.) are not `shared_card` or `todomvc` pages. They'll get colours only until each one's layout is measured and a renderer is added — same rule the zip's `render_css.py` sets.
